@@ -335,7 +335,7 @@ equals_one_over_one_minus_c True</pre>
       <section class="canon-breaks" id="canon-breaks">
         <h2 id="s5"><span class="section-number">5</span>Where the canon breaks</h2>
         <ul>
-          <li><strong>Fixed prices.</strong> A3 holds prices fixed and sets the inflation rate expected for the period to zero. Output and the interest rate are then the only moving variables. In a model with prices, output and the price level are determined together, which is what L05 adds.</li>
+          <li><strong>Fixed prices.</strong> A3 holds prices fixed and sets the inflation rate expected for the period to zero. Output and the interest rate are then the two unknowns of the model. In a model with prices, output and the price level are determined together, which is what L05 adds.</li>
           <li><strong>A static model.</strong> Proposition 3.2 gives one equilibrium at a time. The model contains no path from one equilibrium to another and no mechanism that makes output return to trend. The business cycle of Definition 1.1 is therefore not explained by this model. The model compares two equilibria that differ in one parameter.</li>
           <li><strong>A money supply fixed by assumption.</strong> The LM relation takes \(\bar m\) as given (A1). A central bank that sets the interest rate by a rule, and not the money stock, needs a different relation, which L07 treats as a rule that sets the nominal policy rate from inflation and the output gap.</li>
           <li><strong>A closed economy.</strong> There are no exports, imports or exchange rates. Open-economy models are outside E5.</li>
diff --git a/course/modules/E/E5/L06.html b/course/modules/E/E5/L06.html
index 1e8cf903..163df881 100644
--- a/course/modules/E/E5/L06.html
+++ b/course/modules/E/E5/L06.html
@@ -57,7 +57,7 @@
 \[
   \pi_t = \frac{P_t - P_{t-1}}{P_{t-1}}.
 \]
-With periods of one year, \(\pi_t\) is an annual rate. It is a decimal, so 0.03 is 3 percent. The <dfn>expected inflation</dfn> \(\pi^{e}_t\) is the inflation rate that is expected for period \(t\), formed at the end of period \(t - 1\). The model has no random variables, so \(\pi^{e}_t\) is a forecast value fixed by a rule (A3 below), not an average over a probability distribution. The expected price level for period \(t\) is \(P^{e}_t = P_{t-1}(1 + \pi^{e}_t)\).</p>
+With periods of one year, \(\pi_t\) is an annual rate. It is a decimal, so 0.03 is 3 percent. The <dfn>expected inflation</dfn> \(\pi^{e}_t\) is the inflation rate that is expected for period \(t\), formed at the end of period \(t - 1\). The model has no random variables, so \(\pi^{e}_t\) is a forecast value fixed by a rule (A3 below), not an average over a probability distribution. The seeded draws in Lab 1 generate an unemployment path for illustration; they are not part of the model. The expected price level for period \(t\) is \(P^{e}_t = P_{t-1}(1 + \pi^{e}_t)\).</p>
 
       <div class="block definition">
         <span class="block-title">Assumption A1 <span class="name">(the short-run supply curve in each period)</span></span>
